`catch` handles the complete selected expression locally, including implicit
built-in failure and compatible typed errors.

The expression-wide rules below describe the accepted contract, not current
compiler support. See @../progress (Progress).

### Protected expression

`catch` has lower precedence than the ordinary operators. Parentheses around
the protected expression are not required.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    value = left * right + offset catch:
        then 0
    ;
]

This handler covers multiplication and addition, not only the last operand.
It also covers receiver evaluation, arguments, nested calls and cast operands.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    amount = load_amount(left * right + offset) catch then 0
]

If `load_amount` returns `Int, Error!`, this handler covers its returned error
and both arithmetic operations in its argument. Compatible typed calls inside
the protected expression supply their success values without per-call `!`.

Evaluate the protected work once in ordinary source order. The first failure
skips the rest of the expression and enters the handler, which replaces the
whole expression's success values. Earlier successful mutations and IO remain
visible. A catch is not a transaction, and failure in its handler goes outward
rather than entering the same handler again.

### Compatible failures

- Implicit built-in failure alone is accepted. A bound name has type `Error`.
- One or more built-in `Error!` outcomes may share a catch with implicit failure.
- Existing typed `Error` values keep their message, code and identity, including code `0`.
- One custom error type `E` may share a catch when no implicit failure remains. The binding has type `E`.
- A custom `E!` outcome mixed with implicit failure is rejected.
- Two distinct typed error types are rejected, including custom `E` mixed with `Error`.

Split incompatible work into separate handled expressions or map errors
explicitly. An unbound catch need not construct an observable `Error` for an
implicit failure. A bound catch materialises the corresponding built-in error.

A checked operation, inferred-failure private call or typed error producer
makes catch eligible. A literal or an ordinary infallible call alone does not.
Later proof or optimisation removing the failure path does not invalidate an
otherwise eligible catch. Statically known invalid numeric work remains a
compile-time diagnostic, even inside a catch.

### Block forms

Use `catch:` when the error value is not needed.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    value = parse_number(text) catch:
        then 0
    ;
]

Use `catch |err|:` when the handler needs the error.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    value = parse_number(text) catch |err|:
        io.line(err.message)
        then 0
    ;
]

The error binding:

- is immutable
- is visible only inside the handler
- must not shadow another visible name
- has the compatible error type described above

### Inline forms

A simple recovery may stay on one logical line.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    value = parse_number(text) catch then 0
    label = load_label(text) catch |err| then err.message
]

Inline catch:

- uses `then`
- stays on one logical line
- may produce the receiver's required value list
- binds the error only inside the fallback expression
- does not contain a statement body
- cannot combine with postfix `!`

### Value requirements

When the handled expression has success values and the receiving site needs
them, every reachable handler path must:

- produce the required values with `then`
- leave the enclosing function with `return`
- leave through its error channel with `return!`
- end in a proven terminal assertion such as `assert(false, "cannot recover")`

A handler that can fall through without producing or terminating is invalid.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    value = parse_number(text) catch |err|:
        io.line(err.message)
        return 0
    ;
]

A multi-success recovery uses `then` with the matching arity:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    name, score = load_user(id) catch |err|:
        io.line(err.message)
        then "guest", 0.0
    ;
]

A fallible operation with no success values does not need `then`.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    items ~{4 Int} = {}

    ~items.push(value) catch:
        io.line("push failed")
    ;
]

### Context and conflicts

- `catch` and postfix `!`, `cast!` or optional `?` conflict on the same expression.
- Explicit propagation still leaves the function. It does not target the nearest catch.
- Catch bodies remain statement-like and are rejected inside conditions, templates and constants.
- Comma, parenthesis, logical-line and statement boundaries still delimit the selected expression.
- Grouping an operand without a catch inside it does not shrink an outer handler's scope.
- First-class fallible carriers cannot be stored without handling.

A catch on an assignment's right-hand expression protects that expression
only. Compound arithmetic and conversion back to the target are separate
checks, and the target is written only after all checks succeed. To recover a
complete compound update locally, catch a private helper call or compute and
check the replacement before assignment. Catch-after-loop and grouped handlers
are not part of this contract.

`then` and closed receiving sites are specified by
@../branching/#value-producing-if (Value-producing if).
